## Conventions

<!-- PRUNE ME. The bullets between this marker and {{STACK_CONVENTIONS}} are the
     stack-neutral core. Before writing this file: drop any that do not apply to
     this project (the port bullet on a project with no listener; the parse bullet
     on a project that never reads untrusted input), and merge any that the stack
     block below restates in stack-specific terms — keep the specific wording, drop
     the generic one. Two bullets saying the same thing in different words is how a
     conventions section starts getting ignored. Delete this comment. -->
- Unit tests: {{UNIT_TEST_CONVENTION}}
- Integration tests: {{INTEGRATION_TEST_CONVENTION}}
- Bind port 0 and read the assigned port back; never a fixed port. To test bind failure,
  bind the occupier ephemerally first and point the server at that port.
- Do not split a file for size alone. A split separates distinct responsibilities,
  improves navigability, or cuts coupling. Cohesive files and flat generated data stay
  whole.
- Start each implementation stage by listing the functionality it needs and searching the
  package registry. Report downloads, last release, maintenance state, and recommend — do
  not default to hand-rolling. Adding the dependency is a scope boundary, so the finding
  goes to the user, not to the manifest.
- Errors are typed, never stringly. A new failure mode is a new error type, which is
  public API, which is spec (gate 1).
- Domain values are typed, not bare primitives, wherever mixing two of them would be a
  bug. A new domain type is public API, which is spec (gate 1).
- Hostile, malformed or truncated external input produces a typed error, never a crash,
  an out-of-bounds access, or an unbounded allocation. Test every parse path with
  truncated input.
{{STACK_CONVENTIONS}}

